AI disclosure: I used a coding agent for the investigation, the reproduction and the text of this issue. I checked the results and will answer comments myself.

### What is the problem this feature will solve?

Right now, permissions are either all-or-nothing, or each path must be manually specified.

There is also a lack of scoping on most permission flags. While adding scoping to these flags would help significantly, it would be helpful to be able to write custom logic to scope them in a more fine-grained fashion.

This feature request is primarily motivated by the lack of scoping on `child`. Initially I had drafted a feature request to add simple allow-listed scoping for `child`, but if, for instance, the `python` executable were to be allow-listed, a malicious script could run:

```js
function portable_rm(file) {
  if (process.permission.has('fs.write', file) {
    // unlink file using node apis
  } else if (process.permission.has('child', 'rm') {
    // exec rm file
  } else if (process.permission.has('child', 'python') {
    // exec python -c 'import shutil; shutil.rmtree(...)'
  } // etc.
}
```

By using regex (or other custom logic), we can prevent these types of attacks by further limiting the command lines to known arguments.

### What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?

A script that allows for custom scoping on all permission grants:

```js
import path from 'node:path';

// Helper: path_str would already be an absolute path
function _check_path(path_str, allow_list) {
  for (const allowed_path of allow_list) {
    if (path.matchesGlob(path_str, path.resolve(allowed_path)))
      return true;
  }

  return false;
}

function check_fs_read(path_str) {
  return _check_path(path_str, [
    '/foo/*',
    './bar/baz.txt'
  ]);
}

function check_fs_write(path_str) {
  return _check_path(path_str, [
    './bar/baz.txt'
  ]);
}

function check_child(exec, args) {
  if (exec === "python") {
    if (args.length !== 2) return false;
    return args[0] === "myproc.py";
  } else if (exec === "esbuild") {
    if (args.length < 1) return false;
    return /^--(bundle|minify)$/.test(args[0]);
  }

  return false;
}

function check_net(addr) {
  return /^https:\/\/([^/]*\.)?mycompany\.com\//.test(addr);
}

export default {
  fs_read: check_fs_read,
  fs_write: check_fs_write,
  child: check_child,
  net: check_net,
  // any permission missing here would be denied by default, unless a respective --allow-* flag exists
}
```

It could then be run with (e.g.) `node --permission --permissions-script=./permissions.js ...`

The scoping does not necessarily have to be perfect, as just needs to match what the project knows it requires.

There would naturally be a minor performance hit, but this would allow for powerful scoping that would allow for projects to have permissions exactly as fine-grained as they needed.

On our end, the added security benefit far outweighs the performance hit, as it allows for better security during development. For deployment, we can replace the permission model with containers to remove the performance impact.

### What alternatives have you considered?

1) Using a socket-based permission broker: https://docs.deno.com/runtime/fundamentals/security/#permission-broker

2) Adding regex-based allow-listing for `child` and `net`.
